    "text": "The approach is a greedy car-by-car schedule. Each step picks the car with the least elapsed time, runs a time-bounded Dijkstra from its position, and chooses the unvisited street that gives the best length-per-time ratio, counting the travel time to reach it. The path there is committed, and every street it crosses is marked traversed. A wall-clock guard stops the search early so the output stays valid within the time limit. I have not compiled or run this against real datasets, so the ratio heuristic and the timing guard are untested.\n\n```cpp\n#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    auto startTime = chrono::steady_clock::now();\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    int N, M;\n    long long T;\n    int C, S;\n    cin >> N >> M >> T >> C >> S;\n    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n        double la, lo;\n        cin >> la >> lo;\n    }\n    vector<int> ea(M), eb(M), ed(M);\n    vector<long long> ec(M), el(M);\n    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> adj(N); // (to, edge)\n    for (int j = 0; j < M; j++) {\n        cin >> ea[j] >> eb[j] >> ed[j] >> ec[j] >> el[j];\n        adj[ea[j]].push_back({eb[j], j});\n        if (ed[j] == 2) adj[eb[j]].push_back({ea[j], j});\n    }\n\n    vector<char> vis(M, 0);\n    vector<vector<int>> itin(C);\n    for (int i = 0; i < C; i++) itin[i].push_back(S);\n    vector<long long> used(C, 0);\n    vector<int> pos(C, S);\n\n    // priority queue of cars by used time\n    priority_queue<pair<long long,int>, vector<pair<long long,int>>, greater<pair<long long,int>>> pq;\n    for (int i = 0; i < C; i++) pq.push({0LL, i});\n\n    const long long INF = LLONG_MAX / 4;\n    vector<long long> dist(N);\n    vector<int> parNode(N), parEdge(N);\n\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        double el_s = chrono::duration<double>(chrono::steady_clock::now() - startTime).count();\n        if (el_s > 8.0) break;\n\n        auto [u0, car] = pq.top();\n        pq.pop();\n        long long remaining = T